  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <fr:p>Every residue of the double integral
      <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[         \iint \frac {P(x,y)\mathrm {d} x\mathrm {d} y}{A(x,y)}       ]]></fr:tex>
      can be thought of as a logarithmic or cyclic period of an abelian integral.</fr:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
  <fr:backmatter>
    <fr:tree toc="false" numbered="false" show-heading="true" show-metadata="false" expanded="true" root="false" xmlns:fr="http://www.jonmsterling.com/jms-005P.xml">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:title text="Context">Context</fr:title>
        <fr:authors />
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter>
        <fr:tree toc="true" numbered="false" show-heading="true" show-metadata="true" expanded="false" root="false" xmlns:fr="http://www.jonmsterling.com/jms-005P.xml">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:anchor>2296</fr:anchor>
            <fr:addr type="user">hodge-theory-iii-10.4.3</fr:addr>
            <fr:route>hodge-theory-iii-10.4.3.xml</fr:route>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:number>10.4.3</fr:number>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <fr:p>We will not prove the above constructions and compatibilities.
    The seed of the idea is in the following theorem of E. Picard ([<fr:link type="local" href="PS1895.xml" addr="PS1895" title="Théorie des fonctions algébriques de deux variables indépendantes, I">PS1895</fr:link>, p. 62]).
    Here <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> denote polynomials.</fr:p>
            <fr:tree toc="true" numbered="false" show-heading="true" show-metadata="false" expanded="true" root="false" xmlns:fr="http://www.jonmsterling.com/jms-005P.xml">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:anchor>534</fr:anchor>
                <fr:addr type="user">hodge-theory-iii-10.4.3.1</fr:addr>
                <fr:route>hodge-theory-iii-10.4.3.1.xml</fr:route>
                <fr:taxon>Theorem</fr:taxon>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:number>10.4.3.1</fr:number>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <fr:p>Every residue of the double integral
      <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[         \iint \frac {P(x,y)\mathrm {d} x\mathrm {d} y}{A(x,y)}       ]]></fr:tex>
      can be thought of as a logarithmic or cyclic period of an abelian integral.</fr:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
              <fr:backmatter />
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:p>Recall that:

    
 <html:ol xmlns:html="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
      
 <html:li style="list-style-type: &quot;(a) &quot;">
        A <fr:em><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k]]></fr:tex>-fold integral</fr:em> on a non-singular projective variety <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> of dimension <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d]]></fr:tex> is a closed rational <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k]]></fr:tex>-form on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex>, i.e. it is an element <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\alpha \in \operatorname {H}^0(U,\Omega ^k)]]></fr:tex>, where <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U]]></fr:tex> is a dense open of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex>, such that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathrm {d}\alpha =0]]></fr:tex>.
        Here, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k=2]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V=\mathbb {P}^2]]></fr:tex>, and so <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k=\dim  V]]></fr:tex> and the condition <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathrm {d}\alpha =0]]></fr:tex> is trivial.
      </html:li>


      
 <html:li style="list-style-type: &quot;(b) &quot;">
        A <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k]]></fr:tex>-fold integral <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\alpha ]]></fr:tex> defines an element, again denoted by <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\alpha ]]></fr:tex>, of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {H}^k(U,\mathbb {C})]]></fr:tex>.
        The <fr:em>periods</fr:em> of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\alpha ]]></fr:tex> are the numbers <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\langle \alpha ,c\rangle ]]></fr:tex> for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c\in \operatorname {H}_k(U,\mathbb {Z})]]></fr:tex>.
        If <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex> is defined by a singular chain <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C]]></fr:tex>, then
        <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[           \langle \alpha ,c\rangle            = \int _C\alpha .         ]]></fr:tex>
      </html:li>


      
 <html:li style="list-style-type: &quot;(c) &quot;">
        The <fr:em>residues</fr:em> of a <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k]]></fr:tex>-fold integral <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\alpha \in \operatorname {H}^0(U,\Omega ^k)]]></fr:tex> are the numbers <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\langle \alpha ,c\rangle ]]></fr:tex> for
        <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[           c\in \operatorname {Ker}\big ( \operatorname {H}_k(U,\mathbb {Z})\to \operatorname {H}_k(V,\mathbb {Z}) \big ).         ]]></fr:tex>
        In the case considered by Picard, the map <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {H}_2(U,\mathbb {Z})\to \operatorname {H}_2(\mathbb {P}^3,\mathbb {Z})]]></fr:tex> is zero, and there is no need to distinguish between periods and residues.
        For the general case, see [<fr:link type="local" href="L1924.xml" addr="L1924" title="L'analysis situs et la géométrie algébrique">L1924</fr:link>, note I].
      </html:li>


      
 <html:li style="list-style-type: &quot;(d) &quot;">
        To Picard, "logarithmic or cyclic period of an abelian integral" simply means "period of a simple integral <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\beta ]]></fr:tex> on a curve <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[Y]]></fr:tex>".
        It is implicit that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\beta ]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[Y]]></fr:tex> are rationally determined by <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex>.
      </html:li>

    </html:ol></fr:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
          <fr:backmatter />
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
      <fr:backmatter />
    </fr:tree>
  </fr:backmatter>
